course: dictionary
lesson: 650
title: Who is the baddie?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A baddie is an informal word for a bad person or villain in a story, film or game. Read this from Mark in Bristol: «Proper job, the writer made the baddie talk all posh.» The writer gave a character a posh voice, so baddie means the character who makes trouble for the hero.
explain: Context gives you clues. The posh voice can make the baddie seem cold and powerful, so you know the baddie is the one the reader should not trust. Check by swapping in another word: villain fits, so baddie must mean villain here.
explain: The word can also mean any person or thing seen as the cause of trouble. Be careful, though. In «neither is "the baddies" just for disagreeing», the writer is saying that people who simply disagree are not villains. Calling someone a baddie is informal, and it can be unfair.
fact: A baddie is a bad person or villain in a story, film or game.
fact: Baddie is informal, so you use it in relaxed talk rather than in formal writing.
fact: Goodies and baddies are the heroes and villains of a story, film or play.
fact: In books and plays, the baddie is the villain or antagonist, the character the reader is set against.
fact: «Chinese dragons, lóng, are lucky and wise and bring rain — not the fire-breathing baddies of English stories.» Different cultures tell different stories about the same kind of creature.
exercise: «Proper job, the writer made the baddie talk all posh.» Who is the baddie here? | The hero who saves the day | The reader who is listening | *The villain of the story | The writer who wrote the book
exercise: «Neither is "the baddies" just for disagreeing.» What does this suggest? | *Disagreeing alone does not make someone a villain | Counter-protests only happen at night | Protesting is always wrong and villainous | Both sides are always goodies
exercise: «Chinese dragons, lóng, are lucky and wise and bring rain.» What does this tell you? | Dragons are always the villains | *Not every dragon in stories is a baddie | Dragons only appear in English stories | Every dragon in every story breathes fire
test: Which word is informal and means a villain in a story, film or game? | Chapter | Sequel | *Baddie | Narrator
test: In a film, who is usually the baddie? | The person who films the scene | The actor who is tallest | The hero who saves the day | *The character who causes trouble for the hero
exam: Which statement about baddie is true? | Baddie is another word for narrator | *Baddie is an informal word for a villain or a cause of trouble | Baddie is a formal word used only in science books | Baddie means the hero of a story
